-- title:
--   p. 240 — $V$ consists exactly of its piecewise linear right-continuous maps
-- statement:
--   A permutation $f$ of the circle lies in $V$ if and only if it satisfies `IsThompsonV`: it maps dyadic points to dyadic points, and is $[z] \mapsto [2^n z + c]$ on each of finitely many half-open pieces with dyadic endpoints (modulo $1$).
--
--   **Formalization Note.** $V$ is defined as the subgroup generated by these maps, so the theorem says they are closed under composition and inversion: the source's "it is easy to prove that $V$ is a group".
-- source:
--   Cannon, J. W., Floyd, W. J., Parry, W. R., Introductory notes on Richard Thompson's groups, L'Enseignement Mathématique (2) 42 (1996) 215–256, https://doi.org/10.5169/seals-87877, section 6, p. 240, the definition of V

